 {
  "name": "Clothing",
  "index": 5,
  "evaluationOrder": 2,
  "description": "Clothing category. Build clothing from the innermost layer toward the outermost layer. Every nude choice, clothing state, general clothing value, and concrete clothing item must explicitly set every current coverage state to either covered or exposed, even when the result seems obvious, so no stale state is left behind and no region is forgotten. When a clothing value leaves coverage uncertain, set the uncertain regions to covered for safety. Only set regions to exposed when the clothing meaning makes that exposure logically clear. Apply gender filters only when a clothing item is actually gender-specific in the literal sense: is it made for one sex, or can it be worn by both. Do not decide gender-specificity from social habit, stereotypes, or what is more common. When unsure which coverage regions a state should affect, consult `Tags_And_KV.md` before editing so region logic stays consistent, especially for distinctions such as pelvicCoverage versus vaginaCoverage. Because the clothing layers are intentionally ordered from inner to outer, equal kv priority already overwrites correctly in sequence, so explicit priority is only needed when a harder or exceptional override rule must break the normal layer order.",
